Interfaces are not a part of class hierarchy, even that they work with combination of classes
in java a class can only extend one other class but it can implement any number of interfaces.


Therefore objects can have multiple types - the type of their own class, the types of all the classes
they extend (directly and indirectly and the types of all the interfaces that they implement) also directly and indirectly.

We will show an example to make it easier to understand:

public class MyClass extends MyParentClass implements Printable, Clonable () {...}

All the following are legal:
MyClass myObj = new MyClass();
MyParentClass myParentObj = myObj; // object from type of parent class
Object obj = myObj; //the java.object class thus everyone inherit from there
Printable myPrintableObj = myObj;  //upcast to interface
Clonable myClonableObj = myObj; //upcast to interface

When we pick between implementing inheritance to interface we would prefer interface in case they are representing the same contribution
since interface and inheritance have is-a relation to a certain object. But inheritance is always a stronger force relation thus
interface is preferable unless inheritance is forces a relation of the "Object A is a first and foremost a B"
